  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Individual measure 2021: Action 2 - Crisis Facility</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Covid-19&amp;nbsp;pandemic is having significant negative repercussions on human rights, democracy and the rule of law in many parts of the world. The crisis has further contributed to the shrinking space for civil society and weakened the ability of human rights and democracy organisations to operate. An urgent reaction is required in the third quarter of 2021 to limit the harm done and contain further entrenchment and worsening of the situation in the world&#xbf;s most difficult, dangerous and unpredictable political contexts.This action&#xbf;s overall objective is to promote and protect human rights by addressing some of the most difficult human rights situations in the world, which are urgent and ongoing and cannot wait for the adoption of the new programme. The Regulation (EU) 2021/947, states in recital 42: `The Union should pay particular attention, in a flexible manner, to countries and urgency situations where human rights and fundamental freedoms are most at risk and where disrespect for those rights and freedoms is particularly pronounced and systematic.&apos;&amp;nbsp;The human rights crises facility will build on the success of its predecessor programme, which was adopted in 2015 to respond to this need. Accordingly, the action&#xbf;s specific objective is to provide direct support to civil society in the promotion of human rights and fundamental freedoms in some of the world&apos;s most difficult, dangerous and unpredictable political situations and/or where they are the most vulnerable and threatened. It aims to support actions in a timely, flexible and reactive way through direct awards in proven situations of short-, mid- to long-term human rights crises, where the publication of a call for proposals would be inappropriate or impossible, and/or where funding organisations working for human rights and their defenders prove to be seriously hampered. In this way it addresses the global problem of shrinking civic space, the space where civil society organisations (CSOs) and other groups defend human rights and manifest democracy. Since 2020, the COVID-19 pandemic has exacerbated human rights challenges and has itself become another global human rights crisis.&amp;nbsp;While mainly targeting civil society actors working on the most difficult human rights and democracy challenges within a country, this facility may also support organisations based outside the given country, such as groups located in neighbouring countries or within diaspora communities. Direct beneficiaries may be local/national CSOs or international CSOs working closely with local/national partner CSOs, human rights defenders and informal groups (including movements, grassroots organisations, etc.).</narrative>
  </description>
